From: DKR-R Windows 95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Stop copying eight megabytes of RDRAM the game never wrote

Every graphics task carries an eight-megabyte copy of RDRAM, taken on the guest
thread that submits it. `std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>` value-initialises, so the
buffer is filled with zeroes and the memcpy on the very next line overwrites
every one of them: eight megabytes written twice, once with a value nobody ever
reads.

Timed on the test machine, 400 snapshots:

  zero-filled   alloc 50.9 ms + copy 64.8 ms = 115.6 ms a snapshot
  raw           alloc  0.6 ms + copy 93.4 ms =  94.0 ms

157 MB/s for the fill and 247 MB/s for the copy, which is what a Pentium II's
bus does -- memory traffic, not a measurement artefact. The copy grows because
the page faults move into it rather than happening during the fill, and the net
is still twenty-one milliseconds a frame.

Same binary, same route, truncated to the same number of frame reports:

  zero-filled   period 209.5 ms   render 40.9 ms   elsewhere 168.6 ms
  raw           period 187.3 ms   render 39.9 ms   elsewhere 147.4 ms

-10.6% on the frame, -12.6% on everything that is not the renderer, 4.77 fps to
5.33.

`new uint8_t[n]` default-initialises a trivial type, which for uint8_t means
doing nothing. Off by default where it was measured, on elsewhere, and
DKR_RDRAM_SNAPSHOT=zeroed restores it -- which is how the two were compared.
The choice is announced once, and the allocation and the copy are timed
separately, because the previous entry in this stack was worthless until it
said which of them it had changed.


**And then the copy itself.** `osMemSize` answers eight megabytes, so the
snapshot has always been eight. DKR is a four-megabyte game: scanned backwards
for its last non-zero byte over four hundred display lists, the high-water mark
is 0x3FFF68 -- 4,095 KB of 8,192, a hundred and fifty-two bytes below the line
and never above it. Half of every copy was zeroes the game has never touched.

The allocation stays eight megabytes so a read above the line remains in bounds;
only the copy shrinks. Same binary, same route:

  copy size   copy      period      render     elsewhere    fps
  8192 KB    93.5 ms   188.6 ms    39.8 ms    148.8 ms     5.30
  4096 KB    46.7 ms   138.9 ms    39.9 ms     99.0 ms     7.20

-26.4% on the frame and +35.8% on the rate, with the renderer's own figure
unchanged. Against the stack's starting point -- zero-filled, eight megabytes --
it is 209.5 ms to 138.9 ms: -33.7%, and 4.77 fps to 7.20, +51%.

`DKR_RDRAM_SNAPSHOT_SIZE` overrides, which is how the two were compared, and the
size is announced once.
---
--- a/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
@@ -156,6 +156,75 @@
  *
  * Behind a switch and announced once, because a switch that silently fails to
  * take and a switch that changes nothing are the same measurement. */
+extern "C" unsigned long long dkr_clock_now_us(void) __attribute__((weak));
+extern "C" void dkr_diag_commit(void) __attribute__((weak));
+
+static const bool g_snapshot_trace_on =
+    (dkr_clock_now_us != nullptr) && (std::getenv("DKR_TRACE_CPU") != nullptr);
+
+static bool snapshot_zero_filled(void) {
+    static int decided = -1;
+    if (decided < 0) {
+        /* Off by default where it was measured: the fill is overwritten in the
+           next statement and buys nothing anywhere. `=zeroed` restores it,
+           which is how the two were compared. */
+#ifdef DKR_TARGET_WIN95
+        decided = 0;
+#else
+        decided = 1;
+#endif
+        const char* choice = std::getenv("DKR_RDRAM_SNAPSHOT");
+        if (choice != nullptr) {
+            decided = (choice[0] == 'z') ? 1 : ((choice[0] == 'r') ? 0 : decided);
+        }
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "[snap][alloc] %s (DKR_RDRAM_SNAPSHOT=%s)\n",
+                     decided ? "zero-filled then overwritten"
+                             : "left for the copy to fill",
+                     (choice != nullptr) ? choice : "unset");
+    }
+    return decided == 1;
+}
+
+/* **How much of the eight megabytes the game has ever written.**
+ *
+ * The snapshot copies all of RDRAM because the runtime reports eight megabytes
+ * to the guest. DKR is a four-megabyte game; whether it writes above that line
+ * is a question the copy's cost makes worth asking, and the answer decides
+ * whether the copy can be halved.
+ *
+ * Scanned backwards for the last non-zero byte, once every hundred display
+ * lists so the scan itself is not the measurement's cost. It bounds what the
+ * *game* has written, which is not the same as bounding what the renderer
+ * reads -- a renderer reading uninitialised memory would not show up here --
+ * but a high-water mark below four megabytes is what would make halving the
+ * copy worth trying at all. */
+static size_t snapshot_copy_size(void) {
+    static size_t decided = 0;
+    if (decided == 0) {
+#ifdef DKR_TARGET_WIN95
+        decided = 0x400000;   /* the four megabytes DKR actually writes */
+#else
+        decided = 0x800000;
+#endif
+        const char* choice = std::getenv("DKR_RDRAM_SNAPSHOT_SIZE");
+        if (choice != nullptr) {
+            const unsigned long asked = std::strtoul(choice, nullptr, 0);
+            if (asked >= 0x100000UL && asked <= 0x800000UL) {
+                decided = static_cast<size_t>(asked);
+            }
+        }
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "[snap][size] %llu KB copied of 8192\n",
+                     (unsigned long long)(decided / 1024));
+    }
+    return decided;
+}
+
+static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_snapshot_high_water{0};
+
+static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_snapshot_alloc_us{0};
+static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_snapshot_copy_us{0};
+static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_snapshot_n{0};
+
 static std::atomic<bool> g_frame_drawn_since_update{true};
 static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_updates_enqueued{0};
 static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_updates_skipped{0};
@@ -541,9 +610,6 @@
 
 static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_gfx_screen_n{0};
 
-extern "C" unsigned long long dkr_clock_now_us(void) __attribute__((weak));
-extern "C" void dkr_diag_commit(void) __attribute__((weak));
-
 static const bool g_gfx_trace_on =
     (dkr_clock_now_us != nullptr) && (std::getenv("DKR_TRACE_CPU") != nullptr);
 
@@ -899,8 +965,77 @@
             return;
         }
         constexpr size_t RDRAMSnapshotSize = 0x800000;
-        auto rdram_snapshot = std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>(RDRAMSnapshotSize);
-        std::memcpy(rdram_snapshot.get(), rdram, RDRAMSnapshotSize);
+        /* **Eight megabytes copied per graphics task, and eight more zeroed
+           before the copy overwrites them.**
+         *
+         * `std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>` value-initialises, so the buffer is
+         * filled with zeroes and then immediately overwritten by the memcpy
+         * below -- sixteen megabytes of memory traffic where eight would do, on
+         * a machine whose main memory this note has measured as the thing it is
+         * actually waiting for.
+         *
+         * Timed here rather than assumed, because it runs on the *guest* thread
+         * that submits the task and has therefore been inside the frame's
+         * `elsewhere` -- read as the recompiled game -- since the beginning. */
+        const unsigned long long snapshot_t0 =
+            g_snapshot_trace_on ? dkr_clock_now_us() : 0ULL;
+        /* **`make_unique` value-initialises; `new` does not.**
+         *
+         * `std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>(n)` zero-fills, and the memcpy on the
+         * next line overwrites every one of those bytes. Eight megabytes
+         * written twice, once with zeroes nobody reads.
+         *
+         * Measured 22 September 2026: the fill alone is 51 ms of a 182 ms
+         * frame, at 157 MB/s -- which is what a Pentium II's bus does, so it is
+         * memory traffic and not a measurement artefact. `new uint8_t[n]`
+         * default-initialises a trivial type, which for `uint8_t` means doing
+         * nothing at all.
+         *
+         * Behind a switch so the two can be compared in one binary, and
+         * announced once. */
+        auto rdram_snapshot = snapshot_zero_filled()
+            ? std::unique_ptr<uint8_t[]>(new uint8_t[RDRAMSnapshotSize]())
+            : std::unique_ptr<uint8_t[]>(new uint8_t[RDRAMSnapshotSize]);
+        const unsigned long long snapshot_t1 =
+            g_snapshot_trace_on ? dkr_clock_now_us() : 0ULL;
+        /* **Copy what the game has written, not what the runtime reports.**
+         *
+         * `osMemSize` answers eight megabytes, so the snapshot has always been
+         * eight. DKR is a four-megabyte game and does not use the expansion:
+         * scanned backwards for its last non-zero byte over four hundred
+         * display lists, the high-water mark is **0x3FFF68 -- 4,095 KB of
+         * 8,192**, a hundred and fifty-two bytes below the line and never
+         * above it. Half of every copy is zeroes the game has never touched.
+         *
+         * The allocation stays eight megabytes so that a read above the line
+         * remains in bounds; what changes is how much is copied into it. A read
+         * up there gets whatever the allocator left instead of zero, and
+         * nothing is supposed to make one -- the texture images observed span
+         * 0x20CCE0 to 0x3712C0, all of them inside the half that is copied. */
+        std::memcpy(rdram_snapshot.get(), rdram, snapshot_copy_size());
+        if (g_snapshot_trace_on) {
+            const unsigned long long t2 = dkr_clock_now_us();
+            g_snapshot_alloc_us.fetch_add(snapshot_t1 - snapshot_t0);
+            g_snapshot_copy_us.fetch_add(t2 - snapshot_t1);
+            const unsigned long long n = g_snapshot_n.fetch_add(1) + 1;
+            if ((n % 100ULL) == 0ULL) {
+                size_t high = RDRAMSnapshotSize;
+                while (high > 0 && rdram_snapshot[high - 1] == 0) { high--; }
+                unsigned long long seen = g_snapshot_high_water.load();
+                while (high > seen &&
+                       !g_snapshot_high_water.compare_exchange_weak(seen, high)) {
+                }
+                std::fprintf(stderr,
+                             "[trace][snap-extent] high-water=0x%llx (%llu KB of 8192)\n",
+                             (unsigned long long)g_snapshot_high_water.load(),
+                             (unsigned long long)(g_snapshot_high_water.load() / 1024));
+                std::fprintf(stderr,
+                             "[trace][snap] n=%llu alloc=%llu us copy=%llu us\n",
+                             n, (unsigned long long)g_snapshot_alloc_us.load(),
+                             (unsigned long long)g_snapshot_copy_us.load());
+                if (dkr_diag_commit) { dkr_diag_commit(); }
+            }
+        }
         events_context.action_queue.enqueue(SpTaskAction{
             *task, std::move(rdram_snapshot), true });
     }
